1 Introduction
**************

A _directed acyclic graph_ (DAG) is a set of nodes connected by one-way
edges with no cycles: dependencies, prerequisites, build orders, and
workflow steps are all DAGs.  Drawing one by hand is tedious -- you move
boxes around, untangle arrows, and start over when you run out of room.

   Dag Draw removes that work.  You describe the _structure_ (what
connects to what); it computes the _layout_ (where each node sits and
how each edge is routed) using the GKNV algorithm from the paper _A
Technique for Drawing Directed Graphs_ by Gansner, Koutsofios, North,
and Vo -- the algorithm behind Graphviz.  The result is a clean,
top-to-bottom hierarchical drawing.

   Every drawing follows the same three steps:

  1. *Build* a graph, by mutating an empty one or from a declarative
     spec.
  2. *Lay out* the graph, which assigns ranks, orders nodes to reduce
     edge crossings, and computes coordinates and edge routes.
  3. *Render* the graph to ASCII, SVG, or DOT.

   Dag Draw works on acyclic graphs.  If you hand it a graph with
cycles, you can find the offending edges with ‘dag-draw-detect-cycles’
(see *note Detecting Cycles::).


