3.1 Your First Graph
====================

In this tutorial we will draw a small workflow with a branch and a
merge, and render it as ASCII art in the terminal.  By the end you will
have a finished drawing and will understand the build/lay-out/render
rhythm that every dag-draw program follows.

   *Prerequisites.*  Dag Draw installed and loadable (see *note
Installation::).

   *Step 1: Create an empty graph.*

     (require 'dag-draw)
     (setq g (dag-draw-create-graph))

   ‘g’ now holds an empty graph that we will fill in.

   *Step 2: Add nodes.*  Each node has an _id_ (a symbol you use to
refer to it) and a _label_ (the text shown in the box).

     (dag-draw-add-node g 'design "Design")
     (dag-draw-add-node g 'build  "Build")
     (dag-draw-add-node g 'test   "Test")
     (dag-draw-add-node g 'ship   "Ship")

   *Step 3: Connect the nodes.*  An edge points from one node id to
another.  Here, ‘design’ fans out to both ‘build’ and ‘test’, which both
feed into ‘ship’.

     (dag-draw-add-edge g 'design 'build)
     (dag-draw-add-edge g 'design 'test)
     (dag-draw-add-edge g 'build  'ship)
     (dag-draw-add-edge g 'test   'ship)

   *Step 4: Lay out the graph.*

     (dag-draw-layout-graph g)

   This is where the algorithm runs.  It places ‘design’ at the top,
‘ship’ at the bottom, the two middle nodes side by side, and works out
how to route the edges.

   *Step 5: Render it.*

     (princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))

   You should see exactly this:

     ┌────────┐
     │ Design │
     └────┬───┘
          │
          ├─────────────┐
     ┌────▼──┐      ┌───▼───┐
     │ Build │      │ Test  │
     └───┬───┘      └───┬───┘
         │              │
         ├──────────────┘
     ┌───▼───┐
     │ Ship  │
     └───────┘

   *What you've built.*  You described four nodes and four edges;
dag-draw chose the positions, split the edge out of ‘Design’ with a ‘├’
junction, routed it around the boxes, drew ‘▼’ arrowheads at the
destinations, and merged the two incoming edges of ‘Ship’.  You never
specified a single coordinate.

   To declare a whole graph in one form instead of mutating it node by
node, continue to *note Building from a Spec::.  To understand what the
lay-out step actually does, see *note The GKNV Layout Passes::.


File: dag-draw.info,  Node: Building from a Spec,  Prev: Your First Graph,  Up: Tutorials

3.2 Building from a Spec
========================

In this tutorial we will build a graph from a single declarative
specification with ‘dag-draw-create-from-spec’, and add a couple of
visual touches.  This is the style integrators prefer: the whole graph
is data.

   *Step 1: Write the spec.*  A spec is a property list with ‘:nodes’
and ‘:edges’.  Each node is ‘(id :label "..." ...attrs)’; each edge is
‘(from to ...attrs)’.

     (setq g
           (dag-draw-create-from-spec
            :nodes '((research :label "Research")
                     (design   :label "Design" :ascii-marker "* ")
                     (build    :label "Build")
                     (review   :label "Review" :ascii-highlight t)
                     (ship     :label "Ship"))
            :edges '((research design)
                     (design build)
                     (build review)
                     (review ship))))

   Two visual attributes appear here: ‘:ascii-marker’ prepends a string
to a node's label, and ‘:ascii-highlight’ draws the box with double
lines.  (See *note Node Attributes:: for the full list.)

   *Step 2: Lay out and render.*

     (dag-draw-layout-graph g)
     (princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))

   The result:

     ┌──────────┐
     │ Research │
     └─────┬────┘
           │
           │
     ┌─────▼──┐
     │* Design│
     └────┬───┘
          │
          │
     ┌────▼──┐
     │ Build │
     └───┬───┘
         │
         │
     ╔═══▼════╗
     ║ Review ║
     ╚════╦═══╝
          │
          │
     ┌────▼──┐
     │ Ship  │
     └───────┘

   Notice the ‘* ~ marker on ~Design’ and the double-line box around
‘Review’.

   *What you've built.*  You created a five-node pipeline from one data
structure -- no imperative ‘add-node~/~add-edge’ calls.  Because the
spec is just a plist, it is easy to generate programmatically, which is
how packages such as org-gtd integrate dag-draw.

   To highlight a node at render time instead of in the spec, see *note
Highlighting a Node::.  For the full spec grammar and validation rules,
see *note Constructing Graphs::.


