6 Explanation
*************

These sections discuss the ideas behind dag-draw.  They are background
reading, not step-by-step instructions; for those, see the *note
Tutorials:: and *note How-To Guides::.

* Menu:

* The GKNV Layout Passes::       What "lay out" actually does.
* Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions:: How the box-drawing characters are chosen.
* Coordinate Modes::             Why ASCII and high-res coordinates differ.
* Why DAG-only::                 The acyclicity assumption.


File: dag-draw.info,  Node: The GKNV Layout Passes,  Next: Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions,  Up: Explanation

6.1 The GKNV Layout Passes
==========================

Dag Draw implements the four-pass hierarchical layout from Gansner,
Koutsofios, North, and Vo's _A Technique for Drawing Directed Graphs_.
‘dag-draw-layout-graph’ runs the passes in order:

  1. *Ranking* assigns each node to a horizontal level (its _rank_), so
     that every edge points downward by at least its minimum length.
     Sources end up near the top, sinks near the bottom.
  2. *Ordering* permutes the nodes within each rank to minimize the
     number of edge crossings, using iterated median heuristics.  This
     is what keeps the picture from turning into spaghetti.
  3. *Positioning* assigns concrete x (and y) coordinates, balancing
     nodes under their neighbors and keeping edges as straight as
     possible while honoring the separation settings.
  4. *Spline generation* computes the route each edge takes between its
     endpoints, bending around intervening nodes.

   Each pass is a well-defined optimization, which is why the output is
stable and deterministic for a given graph.  The trade-off the algorithm
makes throughout is the GKNV aesthetic priority: expose hierarchy, avoid
crossings, keep edges short and straight, and balance the drawing.


File: dag-draw.info,  Node: Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions,  Next: Coordinate Modes,  Prev: The GKNV Layout Passes,  Up: Explanation

6.2 Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions
=====================================

ASCII rendering walks each edge across a character grid and chooses a
_semigraphic_ (box-drawing) character based on the local context -- the
start port, the body of the edge, direction changes, and points where
edges meet.  This is what makes the output read as connected lines
rather than disconnected dashes.

   The notable cases are:

   • *Ports.*  Where an edge leaves the bottom of a box, the box border
     gains a ‘┬’ (a ‘╦’ for a highlighted or selected node) so the line
     visibly emerges from the node.
   • *Arrowheads.*  Where an edge enters its destination, a ‘▼’ marks
     the incoming port.
   • *Splits and merges.*  Where one edge fans out, or several edges
     join, a ‘├’ or ‘┴’ junction connects them rather than leaving a gap
     or an overwrite.
   • *Crossings.*  Where two edges cross, the character ‘┼’ is drawn so
     both lines remain legible.

   You can see several of these in the branch-and-merge example (see
*note Modeling Branches and Merges::).


File: dag-draw.info,  Node: Coordinate Modes,  Next: Why DAG-only,  Prev: Drawing ASCII Edges and Junctions,  Up: Explanation

6.3 Coordinate Modes
====================

Dag Draw runs its geometry in one of two coordinate spaces.  The
default, ‘ascii’, uses compact integer-scale coordinates and tight
separations tuned so a drawing fits comfortably in a terminal.  The
legacy ‘high-res’ mode keeps the larger world coordinates the GKNV paper
describes (where node sizes run in the dozens to low hundreds of units).

   The ASCII mode exists because terminal cells are coarse: roughly five
characters per inch versus the paper's seventy-two units per inch.
Scaling world coordinates straight down would collapse distinct nodes
onto the same cells, so ASCII mode instead works natively at grid scale.
For SVG and DOT the larger coordinate space is fine, and
‘dag-draw-svg-coordinate-scale’ adapts it to pixels.


File: dag-draw.info,  Node: Why DAG-only,  Prev: Coordinate Modes,  Up: Explanation

6.4 Why DAG-only
================

The layout makes sense only when edges have a consistent direction: the
ranking pass needs to push every edge downward, which is impossible if a
set of edges forms a cycle (some edge would have to point back up).
Real hierarchies -- dependencies, prerequisites, build orders -- are
acyclic, so this is rarely a limitation in practice.

   When a graph _might_ contain a cycle, dag-draw does not silently
guess.  ‘dag-draw-detect-cycles’ (see *note Detecting Cycles::) reports
the exact back-edges that close cycles, using the GKNV depth-first edge
classification, so you can remove or reverse them deliberately before
laying the graph out.


